Quick question before you start: which calendar(s) do you use most (Google Calendar, Apple Calendar, Outlook, or a paper planner)? I can give platform-specific steps if you tell me — below I include Google and iPhone steps as examples.

1) Basic structure & philosophy
- Keep birthdays/milestones on a separate calendar (or a dedicated tag/label) so they’re easy to view and can be toggled on/off.
- Record extra context in the event description (relation, gift ideas, last gift, favorite things, kids’ ages, preferred contact method).
- Use multiple reminders for each item so you have time to plan, buy, and send.

2) Reminder schedule (use these as event notifications)
- 3 months out — “Plan/decide gift” (good for weddings/big milestones or to book travel/gifts)
- 4 weeks out — “Order gift / Book appointment (e.g., card, flowers)”
- 2 weeks out — “Follow up / confirm delivery”
- 3 days out — “Wrap / prepare”
- Day of — “Send/Call / Post on social”
You can set fewer for less important items (e.g., 1 month + 1 day).

3) Google Calendar — step-by-step (recommended if you use Gmail)
- Create a new calendar: Settings → Add calendar → Create new calendar → name it “Family & Friends – Birthdays”.
- Add an event for each person: New event → title “Ava & Lily – Birthday” (or “David (Jasper) – Birth”), set to repeat yearly.
- Put useful info in Description: relation, gift ideas, sizes, links, last gift, phone.
- Add multiple notifications: click Add notification → Email 1 month, Popup 1 week, Popup 1 day, etc.
- Show birthdays from contacts: Add birthdays to Google Contacts (edit contact → birthday), then enable “Birthdays” calendar in Google Calendar (Settings → Add calendar → Browse calendars of interest → Birthdays).
- Share calendar with a partner or family so everyone gets reminders.

4) Apple Calendar (iPhone/Mac) quick setup
- Add birthdays to Contacts (edit contact → add birthday).
- In Calendar app: Calendars → toggle “Birthdays” on.
- For more custom reminders, create a specific calendar (“People & Milestones”), create yearly recurring events, then tap Edit → Add Alert (custom) to add multiple alerts.
- Use Reminders app for checklist-style alerts (“Buy gift”, “Wrap gift”) that can repeat or have due dates.

5) If you prefer a task-based approach (good for multi-step gifts)
- Use a to‑do app like Todoist, Any.do, or Microsoft To Do.
- Create a “Birthdays & Milestones” project with each person as a task and subtasks: “Plan gift / Order gift / Wrap / Mail”.
- Set task due dates and automatic reminders (e.g., task due 1 day before with reminders at planned intervals).

6) Optional: Notion or spreadsheet for richer tracking (recommended if you like details)
- Create a table with columns: Name | Relation | Birthday | Anniversary | Last Gift | Gift Ideas | Preferred Contact | Notes | Calendar Event Link
- Link each row to the calendar event (paste the Google Calendar URL).
- Use this as your master reference for preferences and history.

7) Paper planner option (if you like physical)
- Keep a small “Birthday & Milestone” section in your planner or a yearly wall calendar.
- Use color-coded stickers or a highlighter for family vs. friends.
- Keep a small “gift ideas” notebook you update after seeing something—this avoids last-minute scrambles.

8) Automations & imports
- Import birthdays from Facebook (if you use Facebook) or export current contacts via CSV and import into Google Calendar.
- Use IFTTT or Zapier to create automated calendar events when a contact is added or when a new baby announcement appears in a shared Slack/Sheet (advanced).

9) Message/gift templates (time-savers)
Keep a few short templates so messages are quick:
- Card: “Ava & Lily — Welcome little ones! So happy for you. Sending hugs and love. — [Your Name]”
- New baby text: “Congrats, David — welcome Jasper! Can’t wait to meet him. Let me know if you need anything.”

10) Backups & maintenance
- Export your calendar (ICS) yearly as backup.
- Do a quarterly review: update ages, add new arrivals (like baby Jasper), and note last gifts so you don’t repeat.

