🤖 Agent: Good question — tea tree oil can help, but it needs to be used carefully.

What tea tree oil does
- Antimicrobial: it can reduce Cutibacterium acnes (acne bacteria) and some fungi.  
- Anti‑inflammatory: reduces redness and swelling.  
Clinical studies show topical tea tree oil (usually ~5% formulations) is more effective than placebo for mild–moderate acne, though it tends to work slower than benzoyl peroxide and may be less irritating.

Safe application principles
- Always dilute. Undiluted (neat) tea tree oil often causes irritation or allergic contact dermatitis.  
- Patch‑test before use: apply your diluted mix to an inner forearm, cover, wait 24 hours. If you get redness, itching, burning, or rash — do not use it on the face.  
- Avoid eyes, mouth, nostrils, mucous membranes and broken skin.  
- Do NOT ingest tea tree oil — it is toxic if swallowed.  
- Use cautiously in pregnancy/breastfeeding and around young children; consult your clinician.  
- Keep away from pets (toxic to dogs/cats).

Practical dilution guidelines (easy, safe measurements)
- Low/sensitive: ~1% — 1 drop tea tree oil in 1 teaspoon (5 ml) carrier oil or lotion.  
- Typical/spot treatment: ~2–5% — 2–5 drops tea tree oil in 1 teaspoon (5 ml) carrier. (5 drops ≈ 5%.)  
- Many over‑the‑counter tea tree products are ~5% (gels/creams) — a reasonable target if your skin tolerates it.

Carrier oils and base options
- Carrier oils: jojoba, almond, fractionated coconut, grapeseed. Jojoba is non‑comedogenic and good for acne‑prone skin.  
- Or use a neutral gel or unscented lotion as the base for a spot treatment.  
- You can also buy ready‑made tea tree products (cleansers, gels, shampoos) which provide safer, measured concentrations.

How to use for acne / spot treatment (step‑by‑step)
1. Cleanse the area with a gentle cleanser; pat dry.  
2. Apply a small amount of your diluted tea tree mix directly to the blemish with a cotton swab.  
3. Leave on (do not rinse immediately). Start once daily for a few days; if tolerated, increase to twice daily.  
4. If irritation appears (stinging, pronounced redness, peeling), stop use.

Using tea tree for other skin issues
- Dandruff/seborrheic dermatitis: shampoos with tea tree (typically 2–5%) may help; follow product directions.  
- Athlete’s foot / mild fungal skin infections: some people get benefit from diluted tea tree applications, but results vary — for established fungal infections, an antifungal medication (topical/oral) is often required.  
- Nail fungus: anecdotal/limited evidence; long treatment duration and medical antifungals are often necessary.

Interactions & combos
- Be cautious combining with strong topical meds (benzoyl peroxide, retinoids, salicylic acid) — these can increase irritation. If combining, use on alternate days or after checking with a dermatologist.

Storage
- Store tea tree oil in a dark, cool place in a tightly closed bottle. Essential oils degrade over time.

When to see a doctor
- If acne is moderate‑to‑severe, widespread, or scarring, see a dermatologist (medical treatments are more effective).  
- If you develop a severe reaction to tea tree oil (swelling, blistering), discontinue and seek care.

If you want, I can:
- Suggest a short DIY spot‑treatment recipe (with exact drops and carrier), or  
- Recommend OTC tea tree products (gels, cleansers) suitable for acne-prone skin.

